{"title":"The Halloween Countdown: 31 days of horror to watch (Polygon, 2023-10-10T15:27:53+00:00)","text":"The Halloween Countdown: 31 days of horror to watch (Polygon, 2023-10-10T15:27:53+00:00)
But an effective creepy-kid movie requires a spectacular creepy kid performer. James Quinn Markey fully delivers in The Hole in the Ground, the debut feature of Evil Dead Rise director Lee Cronin. But the movie isn’t just about a scary child — it’s about all the psychological baggage involved in dealing with one.

Seána Kerslake stars as Sarah, a woman recently moved to the Irish countryside with her young son, Chris (Markey). After an unnerving incident, she starts to believe Chris has been replaced by an inhuman doppelgänger. There’s plenty of evidence that it’s true, at least for the audience. But an erratic, violent local woman who also believes her son was replaced serves as a warning for Sarah, both about how she can expect the community to dismiss her anxieties, and more potently, as a dark mirror of her fears, a suggestion that she might just be losing her mind.","entities":["Evil Dead Rise","Sarah","James Quinn Markey","The Hole in the Ground","Lee Cronin","Seána Kerslake","Chris","Irish countryside","The Halloween Countdown: 31 days of horror to watch","Polygon"],"facts":["The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Evil Dead Rise","Founders, are events useful? -MENTIONS-> Sarah","Mozilla bets on a decentralized future -MENTIONS-> Sarah","OpenAI hosts a dev day, TechCrunch reviews the M3 iMac and MacBook Pro, and Bumble gets a new CEO -MENTIONS-> Sarah","Sarah -SAID-> Slack"]}

{"title":"The best movies of 2023 so far (Polygon, 2023-10-07T12:38:36+00:00)","text":"The best movies of 2023 so far (Polygon, 2023-10-07T12:38:36+00:00)
Elizabeth comes with an open mind, ready to inhabit Gracie and fully understand her. And yet, across Todd Haynes’ May December, we see how nothing is as simple as Elizabeth — or the audience — thinks. Gracie is always more than meets the eye: more emotional, more aware, more monstrous. And throughout the movie, Haynes expertly teases out the two women’s mysteries as they each try to craft an identity around the other.

Haynes’ film certainly has callbacks to other, classic movies about selfhood. But in a way, May December feels most in line with something like The Rehearsal, which also has a lot on its mind about how blurry the line between real life and performance can be, and particularly how traumatic it is to ask kids to behave like adults. It’s something Melton’s Joe is tragically caught in the middle of, and something May December always has on its mind, even when it feels like it’s about other things.","entities":["The best movies of 2023 so far","Todd Haynes","May December","Elizabeth","Melton","The Rehearsal","selfhood","real life","performance","Joe","identity","Polygon","trauma","Gracie"],"facts":["The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Stone","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Oscars","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Tom Cruise","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> France","The best movies of 2023 so far -MENTIONS-> Tubi"]}

{"title":"Dear Disney: If you want to last another century, lay off the legacy (Polygon, 2023-12-12T16:01:00+00:00)","text":"Dear Disney: If you want to last another century, lay off the legacy (Polygon, 2023-12-12T16:01:00+00:00)
Lilo & Stitch was so damn weird that the filmmakers basically hid it from the rest of the company; it ended up making back more than three times its budget, and more importantly, turning Stitch into one of the studio’s most memorable characters. And even The Little Mermaid, the film that kickstarted the Disney Renaissance, was a risk; a big Broadway-style musical of its type just hadn’t been done in animation before.

After all, Walt Disney himself famously pushed for progress and innovation, especially in the studio’s creative endeavors, even when that translated into poor financial decisions. What’s made his studio so special over the past century is how it’s looked forward, trying new things even in the face of failure and taking chances that other production houses couldn’t afford or risk. The company’s biggest legacy isn’t the princesses, the theme parks, or even Mickey Mouse: It’s how all those elements started out as daring projects that no one else was doing.","entities":["Polygon","Disney","Dear Disney: If you want to last another century, lay off the legacy","Walt Disney","Lilo & Stitch","Stitch","The Little Mermaid","Mickey Mouse","Disney Renaissance"],"facts":["The best Warlock subclass, feats, and build in Baldur’s Gate 3 -PUBLISHED_BY-> Polygon","9 great movies to watch on Tubi for free right now -PUBLISHED_BY-> Polygon","The future of DC movies in 2022 and beyond -PUBLISHED_BY-> Polygon","The best movies of 2023 so far -PUBLISHED_BY-> Polygon","How to choose the best class for you in Baldur’s Gate 3 -PUBLISHED_BY-> Polygon"]}

